<div class="three-tool-facts"><div><span>Supported Surahs</span><strong>11 with checked counts</strong></div><div><span>Privacy</span><strong>Local device</strong></div><div><span>Progress</span><strong>Ayah targets and backups</strong></div></div>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-meaning"><h3>What does Hifz mean and how is it different from reading?</h3><p>Hifz commonly refers to memorising the Quran accurately. Reading a verse once, understanding its meaning, recognising a page, and reliably reciting it from memory are not identical achievements. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:5027">Sahih al-Bukhari 5027</a> commends learning and teaching the Quran. A digital completion count records your self-assessment and cannot replace qualified teacher feedback.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-surah"><h3>Why the tool checks Surah and Ayah boundaries</h3><p>Verse counts vary between Quranic Surahs. A goal ending at ayah 40 is appropriate for An-Naba but invalid for Al-Ikhlas, which has four ayahs. The new tool provides a <strong>curated list of eleven Surahs with fixed verified ayah totals</strong>. It rejects invalid end verses and completed counts larger than the actual range, rather than quietly treating any number up to 9999 as Quranic content. Other Surahs are intentionally not available until their reference data is reviewed.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-plan"><h3>How to plan Hifz step by step</h3><ol><li>Choose one supported Surah and read the exact verses at Quran.com or in a trusted Mushaf.</li><li>Enter a first and last ayah within the Surah's checked length.</li><li>Set the number of ayahs already memorised within that range.</li><li>Choose an achievable number of days for the remaining verses.</li><li>Use the suggested daily pace as arithmetic, not as a religious requirement.</li><li>Increase your count only when you judge that you can recite those verses from memory, and schedule independent revision.</li><li>Download a local backup when transferring devices or making major changes.</li></ol></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-revision"><h3>New memorisation, recent review and older review</h3><p>A practical programme separates <em>sabaq</em> (new memorisation), review of recent passages, and consolidation of older passages; teaching terminology varies. The counter only measures newly completed verses within one target and does not certify whether you can retain them after days or months. Revise consistently, recite to a teacher and correct mistakes rather than racing through an arbitrary count.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-example"><h3>Worked Hifz example: Surah Al-Mulk</h3><div class="three-tool-example"><p>Surah Al-Mulk (67) contains <strong>30 ayahs</strong>. Suppose you choose verses 1–30, mark 12 as memorised, and want to work through the remaining 18 over nine days. The tool suggests <strong>18 ÷ 9 = 2 new ayahs per day</strong>, with the next uncompleted ayah being <strong>67:13</strong>.</p><p>This does not imply that 2 ayahs are appropriate for every reader or that a first successful recall proves lasting Hifz. A revision and Tajwid schedule should be arranged separately.</p></div></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-privacy"><h3>How Hifz progress and backups are stored</h3><p>Progress is saved to local browser storage, not an external account. Valid compatible old plans from the earlier tracker can migrate when they clearly match a supported Surah; incompatible legacy data remains untouched rather than silently reinterpreted. JSON exports contain the selected Surah and personal progress. Import validates the version, ayah bounds, days and count, and confirmation is required before replacement.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-mistakes"><h3>Common Hifz planning mistakes to avoid</h3><p>Do not mark ayahs memorised merely because they were read, mislabel a Surah, enter a range beyond its last ayah, or assume that all verses require the same amount of time. Avoid making the progress percentage a statement of spiritual rank. Revision is essential when moving from short Surahs to longer passages. See <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:5033">Sahih al-Bukhari 5033</a> on keeping the Quran refreshed in memory.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="hifz-guide-related"><h3>Hifz, Quran Planner and Juz reading: choosing the right tool</h3><p>The <a href="/tools/quran-juz-finder/">Juz Finder</a> locates reading-part boundaries. The <a href="/tools/quran-planner/">Quran Planner</a> lays out pages across days. The Hifz Tracker focuses on memory within a selected Surah; neither page reading nor the thirty-Juz division proves memorisation accuracy.</p></section>
